'''Sidad Pupa''' ({{IPA|mao|[siˈdad ˈpupa]}}, {{Literal translation|red city}}; {{Langx|tpn|Itápytã|4=red stone}}), also known historically as '''''El Panal''''' ("The Hive"), is a [[Maroons|Maroon]] [[W:Mudbrick|mudbrick]] settlement in the [[Guadazequias Valley]] of [[Araucaria]], [[Balisca]]. The city is renowned for its dense cluster of multi-story mudbrick tower houses—some rising seven to nine stories—built within a narrow tidal island and considered one of the most distinctive examples of vernacular architecture in [[Abyala]]. Sidad Pupa is a [[UNESCO World Heritage Site|World Heritage Site]] and is among the most visited cultural sites.

The city originated as a small [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]] indigenous settlement in the late 16th century, situated on a [[W:Tidal island|tidal island]] that provided [[W:Natural barrier|natural defense]]. Beginning in the mid-17th century, self-liberated [[Kafto-Baliscans|enslaved Kaftians]]—[[Maroons|Maroon]] communities fleeing colonial authority—arrived at the island and were integrated into the existing settlement. The Maroons dramatically expanded the tower construction upward, building the dense vertical structures that define the city's appearance today. During the era of the [[Maroon Confederacy]], Sidad Pupa served as a major stronghold and refuge, its vertical fortifications and underground escape routes making it nearly impregnable to colonial forces.

The city's Maroniko name—''Sidad Pupa'', "Red City"—derives from the deep red-orange hue of the local [[W:Laterite|laterite]] clay used in construction, which gives the towers their distinctive color, particularly at [[W:Sunrise|sunrise]] and [[W:Sunset|sunset]]. The colonial name ''El Panal'' ("The Hive") was used derisively by [[Volisania|Volisanian]] military commanders to describe the dense, vertical settlement that resisted all attempts at conquest.

== History ==

=== Original Indigenous settlement ===
{{Main|Aruanãyára Empire}}
The earliest settlement at the site dates to approximately 1580, when a community of refugees from the [[Burawi capture of Takûakesétyba|recently conquered]] [[Aruanãyára Empire]] established a small cluster of two- and three-story structures on a [[W:Tidal island|tidal island]] carved by the [[Guadazequias river]]. The island, accessible only by boat—provided natural fortification against rival communities and, later, colonial expeditions. The Aruanãyára builders used local [[W:Laterite|laterite]] clay to construct [[W:Adobe|adobe]]-style towers, a technique well established in the region's indigenous architectural tradition.

The original settlement comprised approximately thirty buildings of modest height, arranged roughly in a grid, in order to maximize solar exposure and airflow. The community subsisted on riverine agriculture, fishing, and limited terrace farming on the. Archaeological evidence suggests the settlement maintained trade relationships with other Kurupiran communities in the [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] interior.

==== Maroon expansion ====
The transformation of the settlement into a major tower city began in the mid-17th century, when successive waves of self-liberated [[Kaftians]]—primarily of [[Imo people|Imo]], [[Kimbundu people|Kimbundu]], and [[Waajuri people|Waajuri]] origin—arrived at the island seeking refuge from [[Volisania|Volisanian]] and [[Xacano Empire|Xacano]] colonial forces. The indigenous community, which had itself suffered under colonial incursions, received the Maroons and shared the defensive advantages of the valley.

The Maroon arrivals brought with them both the necessity and the labor to expand the settlement dramatically. Faced with the constant threat of colonial slave-catching expeditions, the community built upward, constructing towers of five, seven, and eventually nine stories within the confined space of the island. Each tower housed an extended family or [[quilombo|maroon community unit]], with ground floors used for storage and livestock, middle floors for living quarters, and upper floors for defense and lookout. The dense vertical construction served both defensive and social purposes: the towers could be defended from above, and the proximity of the structures created a community that was physically inseparable.

The distinctive red-orange color of the towers—derived from the iron-rich laterite clay of the settlement's walls—gave the city its Maroniko name: Sidad Pupa, "Red City." The name is attributed to the Maroon community leader [[Àdìkẹ́ Ogunlana]], who, according to oral tradition, declared upon seeing the settlement illuminated by sunset: "''A sidad pupa, a sidad di nos liberdade''" ("A red city, a city of our freedom").

=== Maroon Confederacy stronghold ===

{{main|Maroon Confederacy|Maroon Wars}}

[[File:Sidad Pupa during the Maroon Wars.jpg|thumb|Artist rendition of ''Sidad Pupa'' during the [[Maroon Wars]] ({{Circa|1714}})]]
During the era of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] (17th–18th centuries), Sidad Pupa became one of the most important strongholds in the [[Maroon Wars]]. The city's vertical fortifications—supplemented by upper-story [[W:Rope bridge|rope bridges]] connecting the adjacent towers, allowing rapid movement of defenders—made a direct assault extraordinarily costly. Colonial forces (from both [[New Burawa]] and [[New Volisania]]) attempted to take the city on at least four occasions between 1690 and 1740, each time failing to penetrate the the settlement's defenses.

As the Maroon Wars progressed, its defenders developed increasingly sophisticated defensive systems. [[W:Secret passage|Underground passages]] carved from the gorge floor provided escape routes during sieges, allowing the population to disappear into the surrounding forest and reemerge behind attacking forces. Watchtowers on the gorge rim provided early warning of approaching columns. The upper-story bridges—some spanning gaps of ten meters or more—could be collapsed rapidly to isolate sections of the city if an enemy gained a foothold.

The [[Volisania|Volisanian]] colonial governor [[Eduardo Larrazábal y Mendoza]] (not to be confused with the [[Eduardo Larrazábal y Mendoza|20th-century military figure]]) reportedly described Sidad Pupa as ''un panal de demonios'' ("a hive of devils"), giving rise to the colonial name ''El Panal''. The name was intended as a slur but was adopted with pride by the city's inhabitants, who added the phrase "''di demonio libera''" ("of liberated devils") to their communal seal.

=== Post-independence ===

Following the securing of independence of the [[Northern Confederation]] in 1818, Sidad Pupa's strategic importance diminished, but the city remained a powerful symbol of Maroon resistance and [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]]. The [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1918–1922) saw the city's inhabitants side decisively with the revolutionary forces, and the towers served as arms caches and safe houses for revolutionary organizers.

In the 20th century, Sidad Pupa faced the challenge of preservation versus modernization. The city's mudbrick towers require continuous maintenance—the clay erodes in heavy rains and must be re-plastered regularly. The [[Confederal Heritage Institute]] designated Sidad Pupa a protected heritage site in 1952, providing funding for maintenance while requiring that traditional construction techniques be preserved. The city's cooperative governance structure, in which all towers are communally owned and maintained, has proven effective at balancing preservation with livability.

== Architecture ==

The towers of Sidad Pupa are constructed from sun-dried [[W:Laterite|laterite]] [[W:Mudbrick|mudbricks]], rendered with a protective plaster of the same material. The iron oxide content of the local clay gives the towers their characteristic red-orange color (similar to [[W:Terracotta|terracotta]]), which deepens to a rich terracotta after rain. Towers range from five to nine stories in height, with the tallest structures reaching approximately 30 meters. The towers are rectangular in plan, typically 4–6 meters wide and 8–12 meters long, with flat roofs used as terraces and, historically, as defensive positions.

The construction technique combines indigenous [[Aruanãyára Empire|''Aruanãyára'']] methods—visible in the lower courses of the oldest towers, which use a distinctive herringbone brick pattern—with Maroon innovations, including internal timber frame reinforcement, cantilevered upper floors, and the bridge connections between towers. The ground floors typically feature thick walls (up to 1.5 meters) for structural support and thermal mass, with walls thinning progressively on upper floors.

On the upper-story of some of the tallest towers there [[W:Rope bridge|rope bridges]] connecting adjacent towers; these are among Sidad Pupa's most distinctive features. Constructed from hardwood beams with plank decking, the bridges span gaps between towers at heights ranging from the third to the seventh floor. Historically, these bridges served as quick transit routes for defenders during sieges; in peacetime, they function as social spaces, with children playing and neighbors conversing across the connections. The bridges are designed to be collapsible—secured by removable pins that can be withdrawn in minutes to isolate a compromised tower from its neighbors.

=== Underground passages ===

A network of underground passages extends from the base of the gorge outward into the surrounding forest. The passages, carved over centuries through the soft sandstone beneath the gorge floor, served as escape routes during sieges and as concealed supply lines during the Maroon Wars. Several passages extend more than a kilometer from the city, emerging in concealed locations in the forest above the gorge. The full extent of the passage network is not precisely known, as some sections have collapsed and others remain known only to the city's hereditary guardians.

=== Decoration and symbolism ===

Tower facades feature carved geometric patterns derived from both Kaftian and indigenous decorative traditions. Imo-derived motifs include interlocking geometric patterns and [[W:Adire|adire]]-inspired designs, while indigenous patterns incorporate stylized representations of local flora and spirits. Some towers feature [[Lucumí Tarica]] religious symbols above doorways, reflecting the syncretic spiritual tradition of the Maroon community. The decorative elements serve both aesthetic and identificatory purposes—specific patterns indicate the family, clan, or spiritual affiliation of the inhabitants.

== Governance and community ==
{{Main|Communal politics in Balisca}}

Sidad Pupa operates as an [[Autonomous commune (Balisca)|autonomous commune]] within the [[Araucaria]] regional structure. The city's governance is conducted through a communal assembly that meets in the largest tower—known as the Assembly Tower—which has served as the community's primary gathering space. Decisions regarding maintenance, new construction, and community affairs are made by consensus, following the cooperative traditions established by the Maroon founders.

All towers are communally owned. Individual families hold hereditary use rights to specific towers, but the structures themselves belong to the community. Maintenance is performed collectively during annual work periods, when the entire community re-plasters eroded facades and repairs structural damage. This cooperative maintenance system has been cited as a model of [[W:Community land trust|community land trust]] and [[W:Housing cooperative|housing cooperative]] practice.

== Demographics ==

As of the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], Sidad Pupa has a population of approximately 5,800, making it one of the most densely populated settlements in [[Araucaria]]. The population is predominantly of [[Maroons|Maroon]] and [[Aruanãyára]] descent, with the community maintaining strong ties to both cultural traditions. [[Maroniko language|Maroniko]] is the primary language spoken, with [[Classical Aruanãyára|Aruanãyára]] and [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] also in common use.

== Culture ==
{{Main|Maroon culture}}

=== Annual re-plastering ===
[[File:Sidad Pupa replastering.jpg|thumb|A man during the 2018 [[W:Plasterwork|re-plastering]] of a tower in Sidad Pupa]]
The most important communal event in Sidad Pupa is the annual [[W:Plasterwork|re-plastering]], held during the [[W:Dry season|dry season]]. The entire community—bringing together all genders and ages—participates in mixing laterite clay, carrying it up the towers, and applying fresh plaster to the facades. The event is both a practical necessity and a cultural celebration, accompanied by music, feasting, and ceremonial observances. The re-plastering is considered a sacred duty, connecting each generation to the builders who preceded them.

=== Music and performance ===

Sidad Pupa maintains distinctive musical traditions rooted in both Maroon and [[Aruanãyára]] heritage. The [[bridge songs]] (''kansion di púnte'')—performed by singers positioned on the connecting bridges between towers—are a unique form of spatial music, with voices echoing across the gorge in call-and-response patterns. The tradition originated as a communication system during the Maroon Wars and has evolved into a performed art form.

== Tourism and preservation ==
{{Main|Climate change in Balisca}}

Sidad Pupa is one of the most visited cultural heritage sites in the Baliscan Confederation, drawing an estimated 350,000 visitors annually. Tourism is managed by the communal assembly, with visitor numbers limited to reduce wear on the structures and preserve the character of the living community. The [[Confederal Heritage Institute]] provides ongoing conservation funding and technical expertise, while requiring that all maintenance employ traditional materials and techniques.

The city faces ongoing preservation challenges. The laterite mudbrick construction is vulnerable to [[W:Water damage|water damage]], and changing rainfall patterns associated with [[W:Climate change|ecological disruption]] have increased the frequency and severity of erosion events. The communal assembly has implemented a rainwater management system, including improved drainage channels and protective overhangs, while maintaining the visual integrity of the historic structures.

